      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The arguments for vouchers often intertwine genuine student choice with political motives, making it hard to pinpoint \"real\" success metrics against pure budget protectionism on the opposition side. While some data suggests competitive schools perform better academically, critics argue the \"moral imperative\" rhetoric is frequently used to mask the very real fear of losing taxpayer control over spending. Ultimately, the debate likely reflects a clash between individual liberty and collective responsibility rather than a simple stock-and-flow economics argument.",
      "source": "target",
